you've discovered

266
00:10:17,785 --> 00:10:19,720
that you've been hiding from me?

267
00:10:19,753 --> 00:10:21,522
-I've always wanted
you to be wise.

268
00:10:21,555 --> 00:10:22,823
-But we'll settle on looks.

269
00:10:22,857 --> 00:10:26,160
-But remember that imagination
is as important as knowledge.

270
00:10:26,193 --> 00:10:26,660
-Oh?

271
00:10:26,693 --> 00:10:27,695
-Yes, imagination.

272
00:10:27,728 --> 00:10:28,395
-Oh.

273
00:10:28,428 --> 00:10:31,398
-And this you possess by nature.

274
00:10:31,432 --> 00:10:32,900
The next most important thing--

275
00:10:32,933 --> 00:10:34,902
---is peanut butter.

276
00:10:34,935 --> 00:10:38,905
-But this, man learns only
with the passage of time.

277
00:10:38,938 --> 00:10:39,973
-More than you.

278
00:10:40,007 --> 00:10:44,878
-I've waited until now
to show you my discovery.

279
00:10:44,912 --> 00:10:47,348
-A rear view mirror?

280
00:10:47,382 --> 00:10:49,150
-Stronger than dirt.

281
00:10:49,183 --> 00:10:51,718
-It is everything and nothing.

282
00:10:51,752 --> 00:10:53,653
-Uh, could you be a
little more vague, please?

283
00:10:53,687 --> 00:10:56,323
-It's life and death.

284
00:10:56,357 --> 00:10:58,225
Goodness, riches.

285
00:10:58,258 --> 00:10:58,858
-And badness.

286
00:10:58,892 --> 00:11:02,763
-Poverty, and evil.

287
00:11:02,797 --> 00:11:05,565
-It's boss!

288
00:11:05,598 --> 00:11:07,902
-It all depends on man.

289
00:11:07,935 --> 00:11:08,902
-What are man's options?

290
00:11:08,935 --> 00:11:11,938
-What man does with it.

291
00:11:11,972 --> 00:11:14,908
-Then it might--
might be a weapon.

292
00:11:14,942 --> 00:11:16,177
-Exactly.

293
00:11:16,210 --> 00:11:21,248
And like all weapons, it must
be kept away from ambition men.

294
00:11:21,282 --> 00:11:22,483
-And Nazis.

295
00:11:22,517 --> 00:11:24,218
-But Father, how can I save it?

296
00:11:24,252 --> 00:11:25,853
-No, daughter, your
hands are too weak.

297
00:11:25,887 --> 00:11:27,320
-You'll get fingerprints
all over it.

298
00:11:27,353 --> 00:11:32,460
-And Zor's thirst for
power is too strong.

299
00:11:32,493 --> 00:11:34,295
-He's spreading south
with his henchmen.

300
00:11:34,328 --> 00:11:34,862
-I know.

301
00:11:34,895 --> 00:11:35,863
-I see myself in there.

302
00:11:35,897 --> 00:11:39,267
-But he would never dare
to enter our territory.

303
00:11:39,300 --> 00:11:42,770
-I wonder if it's worth the
risk of counting on that.

304
00:11:42,803 --> 00:11:43,970
-Or the risk of acting.

305
00:11:44,003 --> 00:11:46,273
-But Father, you taught me
that science is always a risk.

306
00:11:46,307 --> 00:11:50,277
-Ah, but a man of science
takes a calculated risk.

307
00:11:50,310 --> 00:11:52,880
Science is only a
means, one of many.

308
00:11:52,913 --> 00:11:54,615
-There's also fan dancing!

309
00:11:54,648 --> 00:11:55,248
-Ooh!

310
00:11:59,820 --> 00:12:02,890
-What do you, the
viewers at home, think?

311
00:12:02,923 --> 00:12:06,360
-I'm now too old for so
great a responsibility.

312
00:12:06,393 --> 00:12:10,463
-And for such flashy
jewelry, whoa.

313
00:12:10,497 --> 00:12:12,633
-What do you intend doing?

314
00:12:12,667 --> 00:12:15,402
-To protect a thing
of such great import--

315
00:12:15,435 --> 00:12:16,403
-Tense.

316
00:12:16,437 --> 00:12:17,605
---there is only one man.

317
00:12:20,007 --> 00:12:21,408
Ator.

318
00:12:21,442 --> 00:12:22,510
-But he's a baby.

319
00:12:22,543 --> 00:12:24,612
-Ator is the son of Torin.

320
00:12:24,645 --> 00:12:27,347
He was born during the tenebrous
era of the spider kingdom.

321
00:12:27,380 --> 00:12:28,948
-Wow, everybody did
drugs then, I guess.

322
00:12:28,982 --> 00:12:30,985
---black knights in the service
of Dakkar and his [INAUDIBLE]

323
00:12:31,018 --> 00:12:32,352
high priest were sent out to--

324
00:12:32,385 --> 00:12:33,787
-This must be
Ator's home movies.

325
00:12:33,820 --> 00:12:34,588
-Yep.

326
00:12:34,622 --> 00:12:35,923
---former high
priest carried off

327
00:12:35,957 --> 00:12:37,458
the infant Ator [INAUDIBLE].

328
00:12:37,492 --> 00:12:38,725
-Uh-huh.

329
00:12:38,758 --> 00:12:41,428
-This is the part of the film we
like to call "she had to ask."

330
00:12:41,462 --> 00:12:42,530
-Yep.

331
00:12:42,563 --> 00:12:43,863
---who constantly
scoured the land--

332
00:12:43,897 --> 00:12:44,798
-Uh-huh.

333
00:12:44,832 --> 00:12:46,667
---sacrificial victims
of the Spider God.

334
00:12:46,700 --> 00:12:47,468
-Spider God, uh-huh.

335
00:12:47,502 --> 00:12:48,668
-Sweeping down
upon the village--

336
00:12:48,702 --> 00:12:49,570
-Sweeping.

337
00:12:49,603 --> 00:12:51,638
---Ator, now a young
man, [INAUDIBLE],

338
00:12:51,672 --> 00:12:54,875
black knights badly wounded
him and carried off Sunya,

339
00:12:54,908 --> 00:12:55,877
his young bride.

340
00:12:55,910 --> 00:12:56,743
-Getting all this?

341
00:12:56,777 --> 00:12:57,143
-Say.

342
00:12:57,177 --> 00:12:58,812
-Whoa.

343
00:12:58,845 --> 00:13:00,613
-As Ator nursed his wounds--

344
00:13:00,647 --> 00:13:01,482
-I nursed a scotch.

345
00:13:01,515 --> 00:13:03,150
---he swore to rescue
Sunya and crush

346
00:13:03,183 --> 00:13:05,820
the iron-handed rule of Dakkar.

347
00:13:05,853 --> 00:13:07,087
-Who?

348
00:13:07,120 --> 00:13:08,255
-Griba--

349
00:13:08,288 --> 00:13:10,925
-Son of Flogma, keeper of
the seven keys of Pintuzler.

350
00:13:10,958 --> 00:13:14,762
---encouraged Ator, and
taught him the martial arts.

351
00:13:14,795 --> 00:13:17,163
-And Marshall Crenshaw.

352
00:13:17,197 --> 00:13:18,732
Marshall Tucker.

353
00:13:18,765 --> 00:13:20,167
-Things are moving really fast.

354
00:13:20,200 --> 00:13:21,535
-Yeah.

355
00:13:21,568 --> 00:13:22,202
-Enjoy it while it lasts.

356
00:13:22,235 --> 00:13:23,403
-Hey, Jean Kasem!

357
00:13:23,437 --> 00:13:27,107
-When Ator was deemed ready
to set out for his mission,

358
00:13:27,140 --> 00:13:29,977
Griba revealed the
youth's noble heritage

359
00:13:30,010 --> 00:13:33,447
and consigned to him
the sword of Torin.

360
00:13:33,480 --> 00:13:34,715
-No, the Shroud of Turin.

361
00:13:34,748 --> 00:13:35,983
---only Ator--

362
00:13:36,017 --> 00:13:37,718
-Jeez, Tolkien couldn't
follow this plot!

363
00:13:51,132 --> 00:13:53,133
-It's the wango ze tango!

364
00:13:53,167 --> 00:13:54,802
-Whoo!

365
00:13:54,835 --> 00:13:57,203
-Get down.

366
00:13:57,237 --> 00:14:00,473
-Before Ator could conquer
the Spider Kingdom,

367
00:14:00,507 --> 00:14:02,877
he came under the spell of
the beautiful witch, Indun.

368
00:14:02,910 --> 00:14:04,512
-Ooh, Cher at the funhouse.

369
00:14:04,545 --> 00:14:06,747
-A thousand year
old enchantress,

370
00:14:06,780 --> 00:14:11,952
who called forth a horrible
army of walking dead for slaves.

371
00:14:11,985 --> 00:14:13,787
-It's the Aztec Mummy!

372
00:14:13,820 --> 00:14:15,755
-Ooh, how graphic!

373
00:14:15,788 --> 00:14:16,757
-Wow.

374
00:14:16,790 --> 00:14:19,627
-Check me out, I'm not half bad.

375
00:14:19,660 --> 00:14:22,697
-Captured the glistening
charmed shield of Murdor.

376
00:14:22,730 --> 00:14:24,598
-And the secret decoder ring.

377
00:14:27,033 --> 00:14:29,135
-Oh come, those Moon Boots
went out in the '70s.

378
00:14:31,738 --> 00:14:34,542
-At last, Ator
confronted Dakkar.

379
00:14:34,575 --> 00:14:35,742
-Played by Tommy Chong.

380
00:14:35,775 --> 00:14:40,847
-His gruesome, deformed warriors
in a battle to the death.

381
00:14:40,880 --> 00:14:41,982
-Oh, he's been gonged.

382
00:14:47,688 --> 00:14:50,323
-Aided by the incredibly
potent shield of Murdor--

383
00:14:50,357 --> 00:14:51,925
-And its free prize inside!

384
00:14:51,958 --> 00:14:54,995
---the vicious priest,
then had to kill Griba,

385
00:14:55,028 --> 00:14:57,197
who had revealed his evil
designs for obtaining

386
00:14:57,230 --> 00:14:59,933
power by using Ator as his pawn.

387
00:14:59,967 --> 00:15:01,168
-Then he broke
for a light lunch.

388
00:15:01,202 --> 00:15:02,703
-After rescuing
Sunya, Ator faced

389
00:15:02,737 --> 00:15:05,238
the immense, hideous
spider itself,

390
00:15:05,272 --> 00:15:08,075
and plunged the sword of Torin
into the grisly creature's

391
00:15:08,108 --> 00:15:09,042
body.

392
00:15:09,075 --> 00:15:11,645
-He'd never killed that
big a puppet before.

393
00:15:11,678 --> 00:15:14,348
-When the spider god was killed,
the forces of blackest evil

394
00:15:14,382 --> 00:15:15,482
were unleashed.

395
00:15:15,515 --> 00:15:18,318
-Uh, objects in shield are
smaller than they appear.

396
00:15:18,352 --> 00:15:21,855
---its molten magma in anger.

397
00:15:21,888 --> 00:15:24,658
-Then he worked out on
the charismatic Soloflex

398
00:15:24,692 --> 00:15:25,825
of Zontar 13.

399
00:15:30,797 --> 00:15:34,435
-Now rest, you've deserved it.

400
00:15:34,468 --> 00:15:38,038
-Later, when Sunya died, Ator
retired to the eastern land,

401
00:15:38,072 --> 00:15:40,873
where he lives in
the company of Thong,

402
00:15:40,907 --> 00:15:42,777
an oriental warrior and sage.

403
00:15:42,810 --> 00:15:45,678
-Played here by Jimmy Carl Black
of the Mothers of Invention.

404
00:15:45,712 --> 00:15:46,880
-Eee.

405
00:15:46,913 --> 00:15:48,948
Eee.

406
00:15:48,982 --> 00:15:50,050
-Oh, I hate that.

407
00:15:50,083 --> 00:15:51,252
-That's enough for now, Thong.

408
00:15:51,285 --> 00:15:54,187
-Uh, scratching, that is.

409
00:15:54,220 --> 00:15:57,223
-You know, that exercise
works, you were right.

410
00:15:57,257 --> 00:15:58,158
I can feel it here.

411
00:15:58,192 --> 00:15:59,192
-Wait, where's my shirt?

412
00:15:59,225 --> 00:16:02,062
-The muscles are stronger.

413
00:16:02,095 --> 00:16:03,997
-Are we still in a flashback?

414
00:16:04,030 --> 00:16:05,332
-Uh, I think so.

415
00:16:05,365 --> 00:16:05,865
I don't know.

416
00:16:05,898 --> 00:16:06,833
-Cato?

417
00:16:16,077 --> 00:16:18,212
-He used to be a
teppanyaki chef.

418
00:16:18,245 --> 00:16:19,312
Ha, still using that old move.

419
00:16:24,050 --> 00:16:25,418
-[IMITATING FIGHTING SOUNDS]
Hikeeba!

420
00:16:28,455 --> 00:16:30,323
-You should see him
with vegetables.

421
00:16:30,357 --> 00:16:33,093
-Ok.

422
00:16:33,127 --> 00:16:34,293
Oh, not done yet.

423
00:16:34,327 --> 00:16:35,095
[Fight sounds]

424
00:16:39,833 --> 00:16:43,503
-Then they do improv exercises.

425
00:16:43,537 --> 00:16:46,273
-Align the vertebrae,
and release.

426
00:16:46,307 --> 00:16:47,842
-And break.

427
00:16:47,875 --> 00:16:50,077
-Shake it out.

428
00:16:50,110 --> 00:16:50,943
-There.

429
00:16:50,977 --> 00:16:51,445
That'll do it.

430
00:16:51,478 --> 00:16:52,813
-Now where was I?

431
00:16:52,847 --> 00:16:54,848
[Inaudible]

432
00:16:54,882 --> 00:16:55,348
-Father.

433
00:16:55,382 --> 00:16:56,083
[Surprised gasp]

434
00:16:56,117 --> 00:16:59,252
-Yes, my dear?

435
00:16:59,285 --> 00:16:59,987
-Tell me.

436
00:17:00,020 --> 00:17:00,887
-Again?

437
00:17:00,920 --> 00:17:01,322
No!

438
00:17:06,293 --> 00:17:08,262
-All right.

439
00:17:08,295 --> 00:17:10,163
-Daughter.

440
00:17:10,197 --> 00:17:11,432
You know you're
the only person--

441
00:17:11,465 --> 00:17:12,265
-Oh, I know what's going on.

442
00:17:12,298 --> 00:17:15,968
---recognize the
urgency of my call.

443
00:17:16,002 --> 00:17:18,105
-But I can't leave you
at a time like this.

444
00:17:18,138 --> 00:17:20,173
-During a flashback?

445
00:17:20,207 --> 00:17:23,277
---dear, you will find with
the passing of time that you

446
00:17:23,310 --> 00:17:25,478
can't always have what you want.

447
00:17:25,512 --> 00:17:27,380
-But if you try
sometimes, you just

448
00:17:27,413 --> 00:17:28,948
might find you
get what you need.

449
00:17:28,982 --> 00:17:30,550
-But you're the only one I have.

450
00:17:30,583 --> 00:17:31,285
-You and the doc.

451
00:17:31,318 --> 00:17:32,318
-My dear Mila.

452
00:17:34,888 --> 00:17:35,822
-Dear.

453
00:17:35,855 --> 00:17:36,890
-You don't have to
worry about my safety.

454
00:17:36,923 --> 00:17:38,092
-Dear.

455
00:17:38,125 --> 00:17:40,427
-A sly old fox like me knows
how to take care of himself.

456
00:17:40,460 --> 00:17:42,162
These are things a
man has to learn.

457
00:17:42,195 --> 00:17:43,397
-Quit shaking me, Dad.

458
00:17:43,430 --> 00:17:49,268
-And goes on repeating for the
rest of life, unfortunately.

459
00:17:49,302 --> 00:17:51,405
-Stately Wayne Manor.

460
00:17:51,438 --> 00:17:52,372
-What's Wayne Manor?

461
00:17:52,405 --> 00:17:54,975
-That's where Batman
lived before he became

462
00:17:55,008 --> 00:17:56,510
the Dark Knight in
those comic books.

463
00:17:56,543 --> 00:17:58,412
-Oh, they're not comic books,
they're graphic novels.

464
00:17:58,445 --> 00:17:59,045
-Oh.

465
00:18:02,215 --> 00:18:04,417
-Music by the Super
Mario Brothers.

466
00:18:04,452 --> 00:18:07,453
[Grunting]

467
00:18:07,487 --> 00:18:09,055
[Mimicking horse neighs]

468
00:18:09,088 --> 00:18:10,057
-Quit pushing back there.

469
00:18:10,090 --> 00:18:11,325
Ooh!

470
00:18:11,358 --> 00:18:15,395
-Put your swords down,
you're inside now.

471
00:18:15,428 --> 00:18:19,967
-That city's supposed to
be around here somewhere.

472
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:21,335
-Follow me!

473
00:18:21,368 --> 00:18:22,002
-We are.

474
00:18:22,035 --> 00:18:24,103
-We're trying.

475
00:18:24,137 --> 00:18:26,307
Which-- oh-- that-- oh, OK.

476
00:18:30,077 --> 00:18:32,312
[Clearing throat, whistling]

477
00:18:32,345 --> 00:18:34,113
-Run outside and see
what the trouble is.

478
00:18:34,147 --> 00:18:35,448
Quickly!

479
00:18:35,482 --> 00:18:36,150
-But you--

480
00:18:36,183 --> 00:18:37,617
-Go, I told you.

481
00:18:37,650 --> 00:18:40,420
Be careful.

482
00:18:40,453 --> 00:18:42,990
-[GASPS] Later, later.

483
00:18:45,525 --> 00:18:48,528
Well, better 86 the hooch.

484
00:18:48,562 --> 00:18:51,332
Well, a little nip won't hurt.

485
00:18:51,365 --> 00:18:55,102
[Shouting and grunting]

486
00:18:55,135 --> 00:18:57,037
-Use the hand safety
on the stairway!

487
00:18:57,070 --> 00:18:59,138
-Hey, wait for me!

488
00:18:59,172 --> 00:19:00,373
-Me too!

489
00:19:00,407 --> 00:19:01,175
-Hey, wait up!

490
00:19:01,208 --> 00:19:02,977
Hey, I found some
funny wet clay.

491
00:19:05,845 --> 00:19:07,982
-It's raining men, hallelujah.

492
00:19:12,618 --> 00:19:13,220
-Wee-hoo.

493
00:19:16,557 --> 00:19:19,192
-Dad, tell me another story.

494
00:19:19,225 --> 00:19:22,295
-I should've realized
the time was so close.

495
00:19:22,328 --> 00:19:23,563
-I've read the script.

496
00:19:23,597 --> 00:19:26,733
-You must ride due east
and try to reach Ator.

497
00:19:26,767 --> 00:19:28,235
The road's long and hard--

498
00:19:28,268 --> 00:19:29,502
[mimicking pnuematic
tube hissing sound]

499
00:19:31,272 --> 00:19:33,707
-When you seem to have reached
the ends of the earth--

500
00:19:33,740 --> 00:19:34,942
-Ask for Earl.

501
00:19:34,975 --> 00:19:37,577
---and it seems that nature
herself declines to accompany

502
00:19:37,610 --> 00:19:40,413
you any further on your
journey, then you will

503
00:19:40,447 --> 00:19:43,350
have reached the domain of Ator.

504
00:19:43,383 --> 00:19:44,383
-Canada?

505
00:19:44,417 --> 00:19:46,487
-How can you ask
me to do this now?

506
00:19:46,520 --> 00:19:48,688
-It is not for myself
that I ask, Mila,

507
00:19:48,722 --> 00:19:51,625
but for the welfare
of all mankind.

508
00:19:51,658 --> 00:19:52,492
Go.

509
00:19:52,525 --> 00:19:53,327
-Take a sweater.

510
00:19:53,360 --> 00:19:55,728
And be back by 10!

511
00:19:55,762 --> 00:19:57,663
-Hi.

512
00:19:57,697 --> 00:20:00,533
[Grunting]

513
00:20:00,567 --> 00:20:01,868
-That's my spice rack!

514
00:20:01,902 --> 00:20:03,303
-Aww.

515
00:20:03,337 --> 00:20:07,140
[Grunting continues]

516
00:20:07,173 --> 00:20:07,573
-Dad?

517
00:20:11,278 --> 00:20:12,145
-Stop!

518
00:20:12,178 --> 00:20:13,680
-Huh?

519
00:20:13,713 --> 00:20:15,815
-How dare you lay hands
on him, you mangy dogs!

520
00:20:15,848 --> 00:20:17,083
[Mimicking dog barks]

521
00:20:17,117 --> 00:20:20,153
-All of you put together are not
worth this man's little finger.

522
00:20:20,187 --> 00:20:22,422
-And I mean that from
the bottom of my heart.

523
00:20:22,455 --> 00:20:22,822
-Out!

524
00:20:30,697 --> 00:20:34,200
-Help me.

525
00:20:34,233 --> 00:20:37,203
-You know, that hat has
a slimming effect on you?

526
00:20:37,237 --> 00:20:41,575
-I thought that among
your many virtues,

527
00:20:41,608 --> 00:20:44,612
the first I'd find
was forgiveness.

528
00:20:44,645 --> 00:20:45,978
-Oh, I'm sorry.

529
00:20:46,012 --> 00:20:52,385
-Perhaps I've not yet quite
acquired total control

530
00:20:52,418 --> 00:20:55,355
over myself.

531
00:20:55,388 --> 00:20:56,690
-Come.

532
00:20:56,723 --> 00:20:59,258
They're only petty soldiers.

533
00:20:59,292 --> 00:21:02,495
They can't approach
your exalted level.

534
00:21:02,528 --> 00:21:04,765
-Oh thanks, but now's
no time to kiss up.

535
00:21:04,798 --> 00:21:05,965
-Life comes to an end.

536
00:21:05,998 --> 00:21:07,800
All levels are the same.

537
00:21:07,833 --> 00:21:10,437
-Really?

538
00:21:10,470 --> 00:21:13,973
-For my own part, I fail
to see why during life--

539
00:21:14,007 --> 00:21:15,975
-Uh-huh.

540
00:21:16,008 --> 00:21:19,980
---we create artificial
distinctions among ourselves,

541
00:21:20,013 --> 00:21:21,615
because all men are born equal.

542
00:21:21,648 --> 00:21:25,385
-Oh, let me get a pencil, I
want to write that one down.

543
00:21:25,418 --> 00:21:26,587
---makes me sick.

544
00:21:26,620 --> 00:21:31,392
I become more and more certain
that my little visit here

545
00:21:31,425 --> 00:21:33,660
is fully justified.

546
00:21:33,693 --> 00:21:35,862
And will be
extremely profitable.

547
00:21:35,895 --> 00:21:38,465
-You can write it
off on your taxes.

548
00:21:38,498 --> 00:21:42,868
-Do you imply that you
are a conqueror who

549
00:21:42,902 --> 00:21:45,972
claims the spoils of victory?

550
00:21:46,005 --> 00:21:50,310
Or are you a common thief?

551
00:21:50,343 --> 00:21:55,682
-Oh jeez, this has more
pauses than a Pinter play!

552
00:21:55,715 --> 00:21:59,887
-Oh, observational humor!

553
00:21:59,920 --> 00:22:01,320
-God, I love "Seinfeld."

554
00:22:01,355 --> 00:22:03,523
-Let's go.

555
00:22:03,557 --> 00:22:08,962
[Movie sounds fade out]

556
00:22:18,805 --> 00:22:20,040
[Laughter]

557
00:22:20,073 --> 00:22:20,473
-Ooh.

558
00:22:20,507 --> 00:22:21,575
-Ah, too tight.

559
00:22:21,608 --> 00:22:24,878
I don't know, Joel,
I don't think this is for me.

560
00:22:24,912 --> 00:22:25,545
-Oops, I lost it.

561
00:22:25,578 --> 00:22:27,413
That's OK.

562
00:22:27,447 --> 00:22:28,415
What do you mean?

563
00:22:28,448 --> 00:22:29,415
You look fantastic.

564
00:22:29,448 --> 00:22:30,917
I could eat you up.

565
00:22:30,950 --> 00:22:32,552
-Oh, an I could
bite that big toe.

566
00:22:32,585 --> 00:22:33,120
-Ooh.

567
00:22:33,153 --> 00:22:34,620
-Hey, now quit clowning.

568
00:22:34,653 --> 00:22:36,122
You know, what we're going
to do is a little more

569
00:22:36,155 --> 00:22:39,893
lucid version of those
really bad openings we saw.

570
00:22:39,927 --> 00:22:40,793
The credits, you know?

571
00:22:40,827 --> 00:22:41,862
-Oh, credits, yeah.

572
00:22:41,895 --> 00:22:43,897
-That is if Cambot will
man the wall of keyboards.

573
00:22:43,930 --> 00:22:45,098
-What do you say?

574
00:22:45,132 --> 00:22:47,800
-Hey, and will you give us
that half screen posterization

575
00:22:47,833 --> 00:22:48,868
effect too, Cambot?

576
00:22:48,902 --> 00:22:51,772
-No, uh, Joel, what are
we supposed to do, again?

577
00:22:51,805 --> 00:22:54,007
-Well, all you do is
we play the characters

578
00:22:54,040 --> 00:22:57,043
we talked about earlier,
and we run around and stuff.

579
00:22:57,077 --> 00:22:58,010
-Right, got it.

580
00:22:58,043 --> 00:22:59,913
Frolic, cavort, parade,
get about the ship,

581
00:22:59,947 --> 00:23:01,648
that sort of thing.

582
00:23:01,682 --> 00:23:02,048
-Joel?

583
00:23:02,082 --> 00:23:02,715
-Yeah?

584
00:23:02,748 --> 00:23:03,783
-Isn't this kind of fruity?

585
00:23:03,817 --> 00:23:05,852
-Gee, I never really
thought about it that much.

586
00:23:05,885 --> 00:23:06,720
-Joel?

587
00:23:06,753 --> 00:23:08,155
It is.

588
00:23:08,188 --> 00:23:10,190
-Well, no matter anyway.

589
00:23:10,223 --> 00:23:11,023
Let's do it again.

590
00:23:11,057 --> 00:23:12,258
You ready, Cambot?

591
00:23:12,292 --> 00:23:13,260
-Music!

592
00:23:13,293 --> 00:23:14,862
There we go.

593
00:24:59,098 --> 00:25:01,233
[Fighting sounds]

594
00:25:01,267 --> 00:25:02,268
-I thought that was pretty fun.

595
00:25:02,302 --> 00:25:03,870
-That was cool.

596
00:25:06,707 --> 00:25:10,110
-She's still looking for
the ends of the earth, here.

597
00:25:10,143 --> 00:25:11,310
-Ends of the earth,
ends of the earth.

598
00:25:11,343 --> 00:25:12,812
I gotta get to the
ends of the earth.

599
00:25:12,845 --> 00:25:15,448
Ends of the earth,
ends of the earth.

600
00:25:15,482 --> 00:25:15,982
[Screams]

601
00:25:16,015 --> 00:25:18,017
-Ooh, she's in love.

602
00:25:18,050 --> 00:25:19,920
-So the ends of the
earth are this way.

603
00:25:19,953 --> 00:25:21,187
Thank you.

604
00:25:21,220 --> 00:25:23,357
-You idiot, we don't
even have a doe license.

605
00:25:26,525 --> 00:25:27,493
-That way?

606
00:25:27,527 --> 00:25:28,995
Yeah.

607
00:25:29,028 --> 00:25:30,430
-(GASPING) Ends of the earth.

608
00:25:30,463 --> 00:25:32,965
Ends of the earth.

609
00:25:35,435 --> 00:25:37,303
-Let me see, you were
playing a Titleist, right?

610
00:25:37,337 --> 00:25:38,238
-Yep.

611
00:25:38,272 --> 00:25:40,407
-You've got a rotten
lie, lemme tell ya.

612
00:25:43,277 --> 00:25:45,012
-Daddy.

613
00:25:45,045 --> 00:25:46,078
-Uh, lady?

614
00:25:46,112 --> 00:25:47,947
Can we have our arrow back?

615
00:25:47,980 --> 00:25:48,815
Lady!

616
00:25:48,848 --> 00:25:49,248
Oh, Mrs. Lady!

617
00:25:58,123 --> 00:26:00,027
-Hey, why us she limping?

618
00:26:00,060 --> 00:26:01,928
-Cause she's got an
arrow in her chest.

619
00:26:01,962 --> 00:26:04,463
-Oh, I can see-- huh?

620
00:26:04,497 --> 00:26:07,433
-One thing still
puzzles me, Acronus.

621
00:26:07,467 --> 00:26:09,802
-Use the handrails, I
invented them for a reason.

622
00:26:09,835 --> 00:26:11,370
-Why eastward?

623
00:26:11,403 --> 00:26:13,307
Why did your daughter
fly like an arrow

624
00:26:13,340 --> 00:26:14,540
straight towards the sun?

625
00:26:14,573 --> 00:26:16,508
-Oh, good metaphor.

626
00:26:16,542 --> 00:26:19,545
-Of course, I could easily use
torture to make you tell me,

627
00:26:19,578 --> 00:26:21,113
but that would be too simple.

628
00:26:21,147 --> 00:26:22,082
-And too much fun.

629
00:26:22,115 --> 00:26:23,950
-Spoil our little game.

630
00:26:23,983 --> 00:26:25,352
-Yes.

631
00:26:25,385 --> 00:26:27,520
I see.

632
00:26:27,553 --> 00:26:30,990
It's quite obvious
that you're testing me.

633
00:26:31,023 --> 00:26:34,427
You want to verify my
powers of deduction.

634
00:26:34,460 --> 00:26:35,295
-Mmhmm.

635
00:26:35,328 --> 00:26:38,030
-Very well.

636
00:26:38,063 --> 00:26:39,265
Bring me the map.

637
00:26:39,298 --> 00:26:42,002
-And bringing me the
head of Gallagher.

638
00:26:42,035 --> 00:26:49,008
[Bird noises]

639
00:26:49,042 --> 00:26:53,547
[Random noises]

640
00:26:53,580 --> 00:26:55,348
-Welcome to "Death Vally Days".

641
00:26:55,382 --> 00:26:57,583
The driver's either
missing or he's dead!

642
00:27:00,953 --> 00:27:03,255
-Say.

643
00:27:03,288 --> 00:27:04,992
-Made this in shop class.

644
00:27:05,025 --> 00:27:06,592
It's a letter opener.

645
00:27:06,625 --> 00:27:08,093
-It's not perfect yet.

646
00:27:15,202 --> 00:27:17,137
-A Mark VII production.

647
00:27:23,343 --> 00:27:25,478
-He doesn't give a tinker's
damn about anything.

648
00:27:29,982 --> 00:27:32,918
-Hey, did you just turn into
an owl and then back real fast?

649
00:27:32,952 --> 00:27:35,988
-Maybe it was too hot.

650
00:27:36,022 --> 00:27:38,958
It should be enough for now.

651
00:27:38,992 --> 00:27:40,393
You've gotta wait
until it's cold.

652
00:27:40,427 --> 00:27:41,493
-That could take forever.

653
00:27:41,527 --> 00:27:42,695
-Oh help!

654
00:27:42,728 --> 00:27:44,630
-Huh?

655
00:27:44,663 --> 00:27:46,432
-Look out, it's wet
there, I just mopped.

656
00:27:46,465 --> 00:27:49,535
-Ator, help me!

657
00:27:49,568 --> 00:27:51,103
-Prepare everything, Thong.

658
00:27:51,137 --> 00:27:53,373
-With pleasure, my liege.

659
00:27:53,407 --> 00:27:54,973
-Hey, he's washing with Lava!

660
00:27:55,007 --> 00:27:55,542
-The soap?

661
00:27:55,575 --> 00:27:56,675
-Oh no, the real thing.

662
00:27:59,712 --> 00:28:01,080
-It's Marmaduke.

663
00:28:01,113 --> 00:28:05,252
[Panting]

664
00:28:05,285 --> 00:28:06,252
-All right, good.

665
00:28:06,285 --> 00:28:07,453
-OK, let's see.

666
00:28:07,487 --> 00:28:09,722
We've got the cow skin, the
banana leaves, and the dog.

667
00:28:09,755 --> 00:28:12,325
That should be about everything.

668
00:28:12,358 --> 00:28:15,562
-All we can do is pray, although
we haven't thought of any gods

669
00:28:15,595 --> 00:28:17,663
yet.

670
00:28:17,697 --> 00:28:19,365
-Well, it's up to the dog.

671
00:28:19,398 --> 00:28:21,600
-No, Marmaduke, don't use
the triple overhand stitch!

672
00:28:21,633 --> 00:28:22,368
Bad dog!

673
00:28:22,402 --> 00:28:24,803
[Whimper]

674
00:28:24,837 --> 00:28:26,572
-I could be wrong,
but this arrow

675
00:28:26,605 --> 00:28:28,340
might have something
to do with it.

676
00:28:32,712 --> 00:28:33,345
-Yeah, she's OK.

677
00:28:38,485 --> 00:28:42,588
-Boy, look at how
primitive their wet naps

678
00:28:42,622 --> 00:28:43,990
were during the Dark Ages.

679
00:28:46,860 --> 00:28:48,293
-Well, it's a pretty big hole.

680
00:28:48,327 --> 00:28:49,595
I might need another
one of those.

681
00:28:59,772 --> 00:29:04,643
-Got a crazy idea, boss, let's
make a giant cigar out of her!

682
00:29:04,677 --> 00:29:07,180
-Would you quit [INAUDIBLE].

683
00:29:07,213 --> 00:29:09,115
-It is deserted.

684
00:29:09,148 --> 00:29:10,717
Barren.

685
00:29:10,750 --> 00:29:11,683
-It's the best show tune ever!

686
00:29:11,717 --> 00:29:16,857
It's brassy, sassy, it's
a musical humdinger!

687
00:29:16,890 --> 00:29:20,460
-Let's see--
[UNINTELLIGIBLE BABBLING].

688
00:29:20,493 --> 00:29:21,227
Wait a minute.

689
00:29:21,260 --> 00:29:22,562
-Ator!

690
00:29:22,595 --> 00:29:23,663
-This is the Magna Carta!

691
00:29:28,233 --> 00:29:32,305
-So now we have it.

692
00:29:32,338 --> 00:29:37,377
He was another pupil
of yours, wasn't he?

693
00:29:37,410 --> 00:29:38,478
As you can see--

694
00:29:38,512 --> 00:29:39,612
-He's kind of depressed
about his part in this film,

695
00:29:39,645 --> 00:29:42,315
isn't he?

696
00:29:42,348 --> 00:29:45,818
-Many stories have grown up
around this pupil of yours.

697
00:29:45,852 --> 00:29:47,320
-I wish you'd grow up.

698
00:29:47,353 --> 00:29:51,557
-But now the time has
come for me to discover--

699
00:29:51,590 --> 00:29:52,825
-The manchild within.

700
00:29:52,858 --> 00:29:55,262
---the truth of these stories.

701
00:29:57,797 --> 00:29:59,532
-I pledge allegiance--

702
00:29:59,565 --> 00:30:02,368
-This is really amazing.

703
00:30:02,402 --> 00:30:03,837
Why, your knowledge
of surgery surpasses

704
00:30:03,870 --> 00:30:05,472
even that of the great one.

705
00:30:05,505 --> 00:30:07,773
-Who, Jackie Gleason?

706
00:30:07,807 --> 00:30:10,443
-Oh, I'm just tallying
up your bill, here.

707
00:30:10,477 --> 00:30:13,880
-I'm ready to go, now.

708
00:30:13,913 --> 00:30:16,983
-No, you're not going.

709
00:30:17,017 --> 00:30:18,518
I'll decide when you're ready.

710
00:30:18,552 --> 00:30:19,752
-Little missy.

711
00:30:19,785 --> 00:30:21,353
-The journey is long.

712
00:30:21,387 --> 00:30:24,357
-Long journey.

713
00:30:24,390 --> 00:30:26,025
-Once we start,
there'll be no stopping.

714
00:30:26,058 --> 00:30:30,263
-But I explained to you that
my father is in great danger.

715
00:30:30,297 --> 00:30:32,832
-Danger-- how many
a's in danger?

716
00:30:32,865 --> 00:30:35,468
-I understand the dangers
that face all men.

717
00:30:35,502 --> 00:30:38,470
-All men.

718
00:30:38,503 --> 00:30:41,340
-The wise one is very strong--

719
00:30:41,373 --> 00:30:41,875
-Very--

720
00:30:41,908 --> 00:30:42,642
---and will resist.

721
00:30:42,675 --> 00:30:43,208
---strong.

722
00:30:43,242 --> 00:30:44,710
-But his heart is weak.

723
00:30:44,743 --> 00:30:45,812
-Heart is weak.

724
00:30:45,845 --> 00:30:47,580
-That's the reason he sent me.

725
00:30:47,613 --> 00:30:48,280
-Sent me.

726
00:30:48,313 --> 00:30:49,315
What do you think he's writing?

727
00:30:49,348 --> 00:30:50,048
-The script?

728
00:30:50,082 --> 00:30:51,850
-Oh.

729
00:30:51,885 --> 00:30:53,553
-All right.

730
00:30:53,587 --> 00:30:54,687
But you must know this--

731
00:30:54,720 --> 00:30:55,755
-2 plus 2 is 5.

732
00:30:55,788 --> 00:30:56,890
I mean '.

733
00:30:56,923 --> 00:31:01,593
-If you're too weak, we'll
have to leave you behind.

734
00:31:01,627 --> 00:31:03,563
-Thanks for the beer, Gomez.

735
00:31:03,597 --> 00:31:06,865
-But first you must
prove to us that you

736
00:31:06,865 --> 00:31:09,868
are the daughter
of the great one.

737
00:31:09,902 --> 00:31:12,372
-If you can look bored and
speak haltingly, you're in!

738
00:31:16,842 --> 00:31:19,345
-Come with me.

739
00:31:19,378 --> 00:31:21,680
-No, not you, her.

740
00:31:21,713 --> 00:31:22,682
-There she is.

741
00:31:28,755 --> 00:31:32,058
-Too bad Gomez didn't
get a speaking role here.

742
00:31:32,092 --> 00:31:33,125
-Poor guy.

743
00:31:33,158 --> 00:31:35,662
-Go in there.

744
00:31:35,695 --> 00:31:36,695
-Oh, right.

745
00:31:36,728 --> 00:31:39,932
I'm a freshman at a
small Midwestern college.

746
00:31:39,965 --> 00:31:42,435
The small one was
dark with long hair.

747
00:31:46,940 --> 00:31:47,873
-Mila.

748
00:31:47,907 --> 00:31:50,008
-Now sing something
from "Paint Your Wagon".

749
00:31:50,042 --> 00:31:51,743
-If you're really the
daughter of Acronus--

750
00:31:51,777 --> 00:31:52,412
-Kronus Quartet?

751
00:31:55,715 --> 00:31:58,450
---then you will
know what to do.

752
00:31:58,483 --> 00:32:00,387
-Ok.

753
00:32:00,420 --> 00:32:03,588
-Next, on "Current
Affair," women behind bars!

754
00:32:03,623 --> 00:32:08,728
[Humming "current affair" theme]

755
00:32:08,762 --> 00:32:09,862
-Hmm, OK.

756
00:32:09,895 --> 00:32:13,032
Let's see now, there's a
superball, and half a peanut,

757
00:32:13,065 --> 00:32:15,568
and a length of kite string,
and a carpenter's saw.

758
00:32:15,602 --> 00:32:17,170
What would MacGyver do?

759
00:32:21,807 --> 00:32:24,210
-Is this right?

760
00:32:24,243 --> 00:32:25,210
-Mm, mm-mm.

761
00:32:25,243 --> 00:32:25,812
-Wrong.

762
00:32:25,845 --> 00:32:26,478
Cold.

763
00:32:26,512 --> 00:32:27,547
Very cold.

764
00:32:27,580 --> 00:32:28,180
-Freezing.

765
00:32:28,213 --> 00:32:29,615
-Frigid.

766
00:32:29,648 --> 00:32:30,015
-Freezing.

767
00:32:30,048 --> 00:32:30,650
Alaska.

768
00:32:33,152 --> 00:32:35,455
-Oh, you're a cute
one, aren't you?

769
00:32:35,488 --> 00:32:36,688
-Getting warmer.

770
00:32:36,722 --> 00:32:37,923
-Warm as toast.

771
00:32:37,957 --> 00:32:39,125
-She made, uh--

772
00:32:39,158 --> 00:32:40,158
-Very warm.

773
00:32:40,192 --> 00:32:43,028
-She's making flash
powder from her own filth.

774
00:32:43,062 --> 00:32:43,897
-Oh, neat.

775
00:32:43,930 --> 00:32:45,832
[Vomit noises]

776
00:32:50,770 --> 00:32:52,705
-Dibs.

777
00:32:52,738 --> 00:32:53,138
-Rats.

778
00:32:58,110 --> 00:33:00,078
-Uh-oh.

779
00:33:00,112 --> 00:33:01,680
-Oh!

780
00:33:01,713 --> 00:33:04,183
-(SINGING) Love, love, love!

781
00:33:04,217 --> 00:33:06,618
Doot-doot-doo!

782
00:33:06,652 --> 00:33:08,620
-They do have a lot of
product to burn off.

783
00:33:08,653 --> 00:33:10,623
-Mm-hmm.

784
00:33:10,657 --> 00:33:12,725
-I suppose she could
have just tried the lock.

785
00:33:15,327 --> 00:33:15,962
-All done?

786
00:33:19,198 --> 00:33:22,168
-Uh, Gomez, don't pass
in front of the actors.

787
00:33:22,202 --> 00:33:23,737
-Please.

788
00:33:23,770 --> 00:33:26,105
-When I left your
father's castle,

789
00:33:26,138 --> 00:33:29,142
he told me that if ever
the geometric nucleus would

790
00:33:29,175 --> 00:33:33,312
endanger the world's peace,
a messenger would come to me

791
00:33:33,345 --> 00:33:37,883
and prove his identity
beyond a doubt.

792
00:33:37,917 --> 00:33:41,920
-You owe me $50 for the door.

793
00:33:41,953 --> 00:33:43,990
-Ancient Chinese secret, huh.

